.TH SRS-GUI 1 "2026-10-31" "srs-sysinfo 2.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
srs-gui \- SRS system information viewer (GTK 3)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B srs-gui
[\fB\-\-gui\-open=\fIPANEL\fR[,\fIPANEL\fR...]] [\fB\-\-gui\-tray\fR | \fB\-\-tray\fR] [\fB\-\-gui\-classic\fR | \fB\-\-gui\-new\fR]
.br
.B srs-gui
\fB\-\-power\-recorder\fR
.br
.B srs-gui
\fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B srs-gui
is the graphical front end of SRS, a Linux system information viewer inspired by
SIV64X (System Information Viewer by Ray Hinchliffe, RH Software) and built to improve on it.
SRS is an independent project, not affiliated with SIV or its author. It shows CPU,
memory, sensor, storage, bus, GPU, network and operating-system information,
and includes Stress & Burnin (the CPU burn-in / stress tester and GPU Burnin in one window) and
benchmarks (Dhrystone / Whetstone, IPI delay, memory latency, Cache & Memory, Storage, OS Latency,
CPU Compute, Everyday Work, Network, GPU Benchmark, GPU Compute comparison, SRS Pi, Crypto, Prime
Sieve); in the main window each is a tab of the Benchmarks & Stress page, in the classic view its own
window. Nothing runs until you press Run or Start, and every benchmark asks before it starts.
The stress tests and benchmarks are used at your own risk: SRS comes without any warranty, and its
authors are not responsible for any damage (GNU GPL v2). The first one asks you to agree once
(Options \- Load test terms (warranty)... shows the terms again).
The CPU and GPU stop limits of Stress & Burnin go from 50 to 110 C; a limit over 100 C is asked
about once ("Yes, I accept the risk" / "No"; No puts both back to 100), and the folded GPU options
line shows the GPU's own limit.
SRS is not affiliated with or endorsed by RH Software.
.PP
The main window (new in 2.0) has a sidebar of pages: Overview, Processor, Memory,
Motherboard & BIOS, Graphics & Audio, Storage, Network, Sensors, Power & Cost, USB & PCI
devices, Operating system, Benchmarks & Stress and Tools; \fBAlt+1\fR ... \fBAlt+9\fR,
\fBAlt+0\fR, \fBAlt+\-\fR, \fBAlt+=\fR and \fBAlt+Backspace\fR go to them in that order. The Overview answers "Is my PC OK?" with a
status light per check (temperatures against each sensor's own limit, drive health, hardware
errors, free disk space, memory in use, sensor alarms) and cards with the key facts. Each other
page shows key facts and the items as tabs; "All N items" is a searchable list of every item of
the page. The search box (\fBCtrl+F\fR) finds any item by name or id.
.PP
Since 2.2 the tabs can be arranged: add (\fB+\fR, \fBCtrl+T\fR), close (\fBCtrl+W\fR), replace,
move and keep them; each page's tabs and their order are remembered. Options set, per page, the
standard tabs, chosen ones or every item, and the tab bar (one scrolling row, wrapped rows or a
side list). Lineups save a whole arrangement: Basic, Enthusiast, Expert (the default) and your
own, with export and import. Windows-only items and hardware the PC does not have are hidden
unless "Show hidden items" is on; items that come out empty are hidden too after Options \(->
"Check now" or the opt-in "Check daily" (off by default; it never runs the network items or Ping). Click a table's column heading to sort it (numbers by value with their
units, dates by date); Reset view, Copy and Save Report keep the item's own order. CPU Clocks
shows each clock at stock next to what is in effect (measured base clock, FCLK / UCLK / MCLK from
the SMU). The Startup programs tab opens on its read-only Report; Manage, next to it, opens The Big
Top \(-> Performance \(-> Startup Apps (since 2.5 the one startup manager; the 2.2 Startup manager is
gone, and the copies it saved are offered in Restore Removed). A badge in the title bar says "Full access"
or "Limited (user)".
.PP
\fBThe Big Top\fR (new in 2.5; the button between the search box and the menu, \fBCtrl+G\fR, the
Tools page, or Classic Menu \(-> The Big Top (Live GPU and System Activity)...) is a window titled
"SRS \- The Big Top". Its \fBPerformance\fR tab is a task manager for the whole PC: Summary,
Performance (CPU, memory, CPU power, thermals, disks, network, GPUs), Processes (End Task, Force Kill,
End Process Tree, Suspend / Resume, Priority, CPU Affinity, Properties; always asks first; PID 1 and
kernel threads are never touched; other users' processes through pkexec), Startup Apps (per-user
autostart entries, systemd user services and Plasma scripts on and off with Undo and a restorable
trash; never writes /etc), Users (sessions, Disconnect) and Services (Start / Stop / Restart, asks
first). Its GPU, SMI, DRM Info, PowerPlay Table and NPU (XDNA) tabs are a GPU view modelled on
amdgpu_top by Umio Yasuno (AMD; NVIDIA through NVML) and only read; Performance Counters (GRBM) is off
by default (turn it on when you need the GRBM panels). A card that can sleep (runtime PM
on, no monitor connected) is left asleep while idle.
.PP
\fBLive tray meters\fR (2.5) put CPU, core, temperature, memory, GPU, VRAM, disk and network meters
in the notification area as their own icons (StatusNotifierItem over D\-Bus; no libappindicator
needed). \fBThey are off by default\fR: Options \(-> Customize Task Manager (Performance tab, tray
meters)... \(-> Live Telemetry Icons. Left click opens The Big Top's Performance tab; right click
has Open SRS, Settings..., the meter list and Quit SRS. The same Task Manager Settings dialog sets
the update speeds (every 0.25 s to 5 s), the first page, per-core CPU units, keep running in the
tray, start minimised at login and a summon shortcut (through the desktop's GlobalShortcuts portal).
The SRS icons stay together in the settings' meter order; \fBTray Position\fR puts them with the
hardware icons (Normal, the default) or in the tray's first group (First); the desktop decides the final
order (on Plasma: Configure System Tray \(-> Entries \(-> Always shown).
.PP
\fBIR Blaster\fR (2.5; USB & PCI devices \(-> IR Blaster): with an ELK Smart / OCRUSTAR USB infrared
dongle (045c:0132, 0184, 0195, 0134) SRS learns buttons of a remote, keeps them in IR Remote's
~/.config/irremote/remotes.json (shared with that app) and sends them again (Send, Repeat, Dry Run,
Learn Code, Type Code, Import / Export, Test, Reset Dongle). The tab is greyed while no dongle is
plugged in. Without permission it shows the udev rule 70\-elksmart\-ir.rules (TAG+="uaccess") and a
Copy Install Command button; replug the dongle after installing it. Only the 045c:0132 was used for
real (plain protocol, one TV).
.PP
The CPU benchmark pages and Stress & Burnin have an \fBAll Cores (Max)\fR toggle (\fBAlt+X\fR, 2.5):
every logical CPU for this run, without changing a saved setting.
Only one load runs at a time: while a benchmark or Stress & Burnin runs, the other pages' Run and Start
buttons are greyed, and a lock file (\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/srs/bench.lock\fR) makes Start ask when another SRS
process is running one. Every benchmark page has \fBStop\fR (Alt+T); Everyday Work and the GPU Compute
comparison have \fBRun All\fR. On Crypto, CPU Compute and Everyday
Work it also skips the 1\-thread pass (only the all\-threads column runs; the 1\-thread cells say
"skipped").
.PP
\fBPower & Cost\fR (new in 2.2) estimates how much power the whole PC draws at the wall, with a
likely range: the processor (zenergy, amd_energy or a readable RAPL counter) and the graphics card
(amdgpu, Intel, nouveau) are measured, everything else is estimated from an editable parts list and
a power-supply efficiency curve. It shows the cost at your price (flat, tiered or time-of-use, any
currency, or an offline area average), records one row per minute when asked (off, for a set time,
or always), draws daily/weekly/monthly history, exports CSV and raises alarms as desktop
notifications. \fBThese are estimates, not a meter reading or a bill.\fR
.PP
The classic view is the SIV-style interface of SRS 1.1: a main window with SIV's button row and
every item in its own panel. Open it with Menu \(-> Classic View, or start in it with
\fB\-\-gui\-classic\fR or the option "Start in classic view".
.PP
Root-only data (SMBIOS serials, DIMM SPD, SMART, ACPI tables, MSRs, and since 2.4 the memory
controller registers, the VBIOS image, GPIO pins, RAPL power, the EBDA and UEFI tables) is read
from a cache filled by
.BR srs-root (1).
.PP
The \fBMemory Timings\fR tab (2.4, Memory page) shows, on AMD Ryzen 7000 / 9000 desktop processors
with the ryzen_smu driver, the live DDR5 timings of each channel, MEMCLK / UCLK / FCLK, the SoC
voltages and the memory controller settings, the BIOS memory settings from the AMD AOD table when the
kernel allows reading it (iomem=relaxed on kernels with IO_STRICT_DEVMEM; SRS never asks you to set
it), and for every DDR5 module its maker, part number, temperature and JEDEC / EXPO / XMP profiles.
Intel Core 6th gen to Core Ultra: experimental. Everything on it is only read.
.PP
Two options are off by default and ask first; they apply from the next Run as root:
\fBRead DIMM voltages from their PMIC chips\fR (each DDR5 module's PMIC registers read over SMBus
with i2cdump, nothing written; a collision with the BIOS or the board's controller on that bus can,
rarely, upset a sensor or the lighting until the next boot) and \fBRead the embedded controller\fR
(its 256 registers through ec_sys loaded read-only; on some laptops a read clears a pending event).
.PP
The Tools page has \fBUpdate hardware lists\fR (downloads pci.ids, usb.ids and pnp.ids from their
official sources into ~/.local/share/srs/lists only when you ask; the newer of these and the
system's copies is used) and \fBACPI method evaluation\fR for experts (also Menu \(-> ACPI method
evaluation (experts)...): it evaluates one ACPI method you type, as root, through the acpi_call
module and
.BR "srs-root \-\-acpi\-eval" ,
and logs every call to ~/.cache/srs/root/acpi\-calls.txt. \fBIt runs BIOS code and can change
hardware state\fR; it stays off until you accept a warning once.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-gui\-open= PANEL
Open the named items or tools at start: in the main window an item opens on its page, in the
classic view as its own panel. For example
.BR sensors ", " processors ", " burnin ", " stress ", " bench ", " ipi ", " latency ", " pi ", " crypto ", " prime ", " cachemem ", " storagebench ", " oslatency ", " compute ", " work ", " netbench ", " gpubench ", " gpuburn ", " gpucompare ", " cudatoolkit ", " lists ", " acpieval ", " idlookup ", " find ", " gputop " (The Big Top), " traysettings " (Task Manager Settings).
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-tray
start in the system tray.
.TP
.B \-\-tray
the same as \fB\-\-gui\-tray\fR (2.5): start with only the tray icons (SRS's own and / or the live tray meters).
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-classic
start in the classic view: the SIV-style main window with its button row, every item in its own panel.
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-new
start in the main window with the sidebar (Overview and one page per category), even when
"Start in classic view" is set in the options.
.TP
.B \-\-gui\-open=power\-cost
open the Power & Cost page.
.TP
.B \-\-power\-recorder
record Power & Cost history with no window, following the recording setting on the page (used by
the optional systemd user service ~/.config/systemd/user/srs-power-recorder.service, which the
page's Setup tab writes and removes). Only one process records at a time.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-\-version
Print the command-line help or the version and exit (these are shared with
.BR sysreport-search (1)).
.SH NVIDIA CUDA TOOLKIT
The NVIDIA CUDA toolkit is NVIDIA's free set of developer tools for NVIDIA graphics cards; SRS can
use one small part of it, the runtime compiler (NVRTC). Since 2.3 SRS's CUDA tests are built in
(PTX), so it is optional: NVRTC is used only when the driver refuses them. The first time GPU
Burnin or the GPU Compute comparison runs CUDA without NVRTC, SRS asks once (\fBInstall...\fR,
\fBNot now\fR, \fBDon't ask again\fR); it can be installed later from \fBOptions \(-> NVIDIA CUDA
toolkit for GPU tests...\fR, which shows the state and the exact install command (Arch: pacman -S
--needed cuda; Debian/Ubuntu: apt-get install libnvrtc12 or libnvrtc11.2; others: instructions)
and runs it in a terminal window with sudo (or pkexec) only after you confirm. With ZLUDA it is
not recommended (it can break or remove ZLUDA): SRS warns and asks twice.
.SH USER GUIDE
Every tab and panel has a \fBGuide\fR button (also \fBF1\fR, and "Open guide for this item"
in its right-click menu) that opens the user guide at that item's section: in
Okular, Evince or Papers at the right page, otherwise the HTML guide in the web
browser. The Big Top's \fBGuide\fR button and \fBF1\fR open the section of the tab or Performance
page shown, and the Task Manager Settings dialog has its own Guide button. \fBMenu \(-> User Guide\fR opens the start; \fBShift+F1\fR opens the Help panel.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SRS_OPEN
panels to open at start, as for \fB\-\-gui\-open\fR.
.TP
.B SRS_DOC_DIR
directory holding the user guide (default /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo).
.TP
.BR SRS_POWER_DB ", " SRS_POWER_INI
use another Power & Cost history database or settings file (for tests).
.TP
.B SRS_DATA_DIR
directory holding power-zip-rates.tsv (default: next to the program, then /usr/share/srs-sysinfo).
.TP
.B SRS_CTK_DRY_RUN
when set to 1, the NVIDIA CUDA toolkit install shows the command it would run and runs nothing (tests).
.TP
.B SRS_RSMU_DIR
read the ryzen_smu files from this directory instead of /sys/kernel/ryzen_smu_drv (tests).
.TP
.B SRS_GPUBURN_SIMPLE
when set, GPU Burnin uses the older tiled matrix multiply instead of the 2.4 register\-blocked one (for comparing).
.TP
.B SRS_SPD_DIR
read the DDR5 modules' SPD from this directory instead of /sys/bus/i2c/drivers/spd5118 (tests).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/SRS-User-Guide.pdf, /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/html/SRS-User-Guide.html
the user guide (PDF and HTML), with its Markdown sources in the same folder.
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/SRSDEF.ini
settings (theme, update interval, tray, "Classic View=1" for Start in classic view,
"Ask CUDA Toolkit=0" after Don't ask again, "Stress CPU Limit", "Accepted High Temperature Limits",
"Read DIMM PMIC", "Read EC", "Accepted ACPI Evaluation", ...); group [Task Manager] (Update Speed,
Start Page, Per Core Units, Telemetry Icons (0 = off, the default), Telemetry Meters, Disk Space
Mount, Summon Shortcut, Tray Position) and [GPU Top] Update ms (The Big Top's update speed).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/root/
root-only data collected by srs-root, and acpi\-calls.txt, the log of ACPI method evaluation.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/srs/lists/
pci.ids, usb.ids and pnp.ids downloaded by Update hardware lists (the previous copies as .old).
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/lineups/*.srslineup
your own tab lineups (the same format as Export lineup).
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/startup-backup/
only from SRS 2.2\-2.4: files the old Startup manager saved; Startup Apps' Restore Removed brings them
back as copies (nothing there is deleted).
.TP
.I ~/.config/irremote/remotes.json, remotes.json.before\-srs
IR Blaster's codes, shared with the IR Remote app, and the one-time copy from before SRS first
changed the file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/.srs-trash/, ~/.local/state/srs/autostart-undo.log
entries removed in Startup Apps (Restore Removed brings them back) and its Undo history (the last 50
changes); the first change to an autostart file of yours keeps \fIname\fR.desktop.srs\-bak once.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/empty-items.txt
items that had nothing to show on this PC at the last check (made only by Options > Check now or
Check daily, which is off by default; the network items and Ping are never checked).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/burnin\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.txt
the summary of each finished Stress & Burnin run.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/srs/bench\-history.tsv
one line per finished benchmark run: Cache & Memory, Storage, OS Latency, CPU Compute, Everyday Work,
Network, GPU Benchmark, GPU Burnin, GPU Compute comparison, SRS Pi, Crypto and Prime Sieve (time, test,
settings, result, build).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/srs/power.db
Power & Cost history (SQLite, one row per minute, mode 0600), with power.db.lock beside it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/srs/power.ini
Power & Cost settings: parts, power supply, price, recording, alarms.
.TP
.I /usr/share/srs-sysinfo/power-zip-rates.tsv
US ZIP code to electric utility table (NREL, CC BY 4.0) for the offline price lookup.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids, /usr/share/hwdata/usb.ids, /usr/share/hwdata/pnp.ids
device name databases (the newer copies in ~/.local/share/srs/lists are used instead when present).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sysreport-search (1),
.BR srs-root (1),
.BR srs-sysreport (1)
.SH HOMEPAGE
https://github.com/eugenesflanagin-spec/srs-sysinfo
